  "instruction": "In 2022, which states who were in the top 5 for death rates related to heart disease, also had chronic lower respiratory diseases in their top 5 leading causes of death in 2022. Use data from the CDC.",
  "answer": "Based on CDC data from 2022, here's the intersection:\n\n**Top 5 states by heart disease death rates (age-adjusted per 100,000):**\n1. Mississippi — 236.5\n2. Louisiana — 220\n3. Tennessee — 210\n4. West Virginia — 205.3\n5. Kentucky — 196.9\n\n**States with chronic lower respiratory diseases (CLRD) in their top 5 leading causes of death in 2022:**\n- Alabama (4th)\n- Arkansas (3rd)\n- Kentucky (3rd)\n- Louisiana (5th)\n- Mississippi (3rd)\n- Oklahoma (4th)\n- Tennessee (4th)\n- West Virginia (4th)\n- Wyoming (3rd)\n\n**Intersection — all 5 top heart disease states also had CLRD in their top 5:**\n| State | Heart Disease Rank | CLRD Rank in Leading Causes |\n|-------|-------------------|----------------------------|\n| Mississippi | #1 | 3rd |\n| Louisiana | #2 | 5th |\n| Tennessee | #3 | 4th |\n| West Virginia | #4 | 4th |\n| Kentucky | #5 | 3rd |\n\n**Answer:** All five states with the highest heart disease death rates in 2022 — **Mississippi, Louisiana, Tennessee, West Virginia, and Kentucky** — also had chronic lower respiratory diseases among their top 5 leading causes of death that year.\n\nSources: CDC WONDER / NCHS \"Stats of the States\" via Becker's Cardiology (heart disease rates) and Becker's Oncology/leading causes by state (CLRD rankings).",
